-- title:
--   Degree over k(t) does not drop under coefficient extension
-- statement:
--   Let $k_0$ and $k$ be fields and $\sigma : k_0 \to k$ a ring homomorphism. Let $F_0$ be an intermediate field of $k_0 \subseteq k_0((q))$ (the Laurent series field over $k_0$) and let $F$ be an intermediate field of $k \subseteq k((q))$. Let $\iota : F_0 \to F$ be a ring homomorphism which is compatible with coefficientwise application of $\sigma$, in the sense that for every $x \in F_0$ the Laurent series underlying $\iota(x)$ is `coeffMap σ` applied to the Laurent series underlying $x$, where `coeffMap σ` is the ring homomorphism $k_0((q)) \to k((q))$ obtained by applying $\sigma$ to each coefficient. Let $t_0 \in F_0$ be transcendental over $k_0$, and assume that $F$ is finite-dimensional over the subfield $k\langle \iota(t_0)\rangle$ of $F$ generated by $k$ and $\iota(t_0)$. Then the rank of $F_0$ over the subfield $k_0\langle t_0 \rangle$ of $F_0$ generated by $k_0$ and $t_0$ is at most the rank of $F$ over $k\langle \iota(t_0)\rangle$; ranks are `Module.finrank`, so an infinite left-hand side is read as $0$ and the inequality is then vacuous.
--
--   This is the lower bound half of the statement that the degree of a Laurent-series subfield over the rational subfield generated by a transcendental element is unchanged by extension of the field of constants; only the inequality, not linear disjointness or a spanning statement, is asserted. It is used in the computation of the degree of the function field of $X_1$ over the rational function field after passage to an algebraically closed field of constants.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_finrank_adjoin_simple_le_finrank_adjoin_simple_of_coe_eq_coeffMap.lean

